🔷 2. Data Construction
2.1 Unit of analysis
	•	ZIP code × Year panel
	•	States: Illinois, Iowa, Michigan
	•	Years: 1973–1992

2.2 Crime data

We cleaned and merged raw crime count datasets into ZIP–year form, computing:
	•	violent_rate_100k
	•	property_rate_100k
	•	total_rate_100k

2.3 Control variables

State-level controls were merged into ZIP-level observations:
	•	unemployment rate
	•	demographics (% Black, % HSGrad, % Hispanic)
	•	income
	•	age

Some ZIP-years have missing controls (early years).
These are automatically removed by FEOLS.

2.4 Treatment coding

✔ treat_start = first year of governor’s party switch
	•	Illinois → switched in 1977 (Dem → Rep)
	•	Michigan → switched in 1983 (Rep → Dem)
	•	Iowa → never switches (always Dem)

✔ treated = 1 if year ≥ treat_start, else 0

✔ event_time = year - treat_start

⸻

🔷 3. Why the initial approach was wrong

Originally we estimated:
crime_rate ~ Gov_Rep + controls | city_id + year

But Gov_Rep is NOT a valid treatment variable because:

1. Treatment is staggered

Illinois switches in 1977, Michigan in 1983, Iowa never.

2. Michigan switches in the opposite direction

Illinois: D → R
Michigan: R → D
This breaks standard DID interpretation.

3. TWFE models are biased under staggered adoption

Based on Goodman-Bacon (2021), Sun & Abraham (2020):
	•	units that switch earlier act as control for later switchers
	•	Iowa (never treated) is the only valid permanent control
	•	TWFE mixes treated and untreated incorrectly
	•	opposite-direction switches create negative weights

Thus Gov_Rep should NOT be used as a treatment dummy.

➡ We switched to Sun & Abraham and correct event studies.

⸻

🔷 4. Correct Econometric Strategy

We estimated three models.

⸻

⭐ 4.1 TWFE (baseline)

Formula:
crime_rate ~ treated + controls | city_id + year

Purpose:
	•	simple and familiar
	•	gives a baseline
	•	but biased, since treatment is staggered
	•	use only for comparison, not causal interpretation

Result:
➡ No significant effect of party switch on any crime category.

⸻

⭐ 4.2 Event Study (TWFE, using i(event_time))

Purpose:
	•	check parallel pre-trends
	•	visualize dynamic effects
	•	illustrate treatment effect over time

We estimated:
crime_rate ~ i(event_time, ref = -1) + controls | FE
Findings:
	•	pre-trends are flat → parallel trends validated
	•	no systematic post-treatment changes
	•	effects ≈ 0, not statistically significant

⸻

⭐ 4.3 Sun & Abraham (2020) — Correct staggered DiD

This is the correct method for our design.
crime_rate ~ sunab(treat_start, year) | city_id + year
Advantages:
	•	correct weighting
	•	avoids contamination from other treated states
	•	compares treated states to never-treated Iowa
	•	valid dynamic effects

Findings match TWFE + Event Study:
	•	coefficients around zero
	•	no significant effect
	•	large standard errors due to only 3 clusters

⸻

🔷 5. Summary of Findings

Across all models:

✔ Crime does not respond to governor party switches

✔ No pre-treatment differences

✔ No significant post-treatment effect

✔ Illinois, Michigan, and Iowa follow similar trends

✔ Effects are statistically indistinguishable from zero

Interpretation:

Crime trends appear driven by long-term structural factors, not short-term political party changes.

